.TH FLEET-TIMERS 1 "2026-11-03" "cti-agent 1.4" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-timers \- list the fleet's own timers, and nothing else systemctl can do
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fleet\-timers
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RI [ cti\-agent\-PATTERN ...]
.br
.B fleet\-timers \-h
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-timers
.I options
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fleet\-timers
is how the orchestrator heartbeat checks that no timer has been disabled, the
failure that raises no alarm on its own. It is a restricted front end to
.B systemctl list\-timers
(see
.BR systemctl (1))
and is installed root-owned in
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin ,
where
.BR run\-checkin (1)
checks it with the rest of the code.
.PP
The heartbeat used to be granted
.B systemctl list\-timers
with any further arguments. That prefix cannot become
.B stop
or
.BR disable ,
but it still accepts every global option of
.BR systemctl :
.B \-\-host=user@box
runs the query over
.BR ssh (1)
on any machine, as the service account, and
.B \-\-machine
reaches containers. A beat steered by a hostile advisory could have used that
to make outbound connections from the fleet's box. The wrapper accepts only
the options below, admits only the fleet's own units, and passes the patterns
to
.B systemctl
after
.B \-\-
so that none of them can be read as an option. It always adds
.BR \-\-no\-pager .
.PP
Anything else is refused before
.B systemctl
runs: a usage line goes to standard error and the exit status is 2. The
operator, who has a shell, uses
.B systemctl list\-timers
directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Include inactive timers, so a timer that has been disabled still shows, with
an empty LAST column if it never ran.
.TP
.I cti\-agent\-PATTERN
Show timers matching this pattern. Repeatable. It must begin with
.B cti\-agent\-
and contain only letters, digits and
.BR @ ,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.B \-
and
.BR * ;
globs are allowed. With no pattern the wrapper shows
.BR cti\-agent\-* .
Any other pattern is refused.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage line to standard error and exit 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B systemctl
ran and succeeded (it replaces the wrapper, so its status is the wrapper's).
.TP
.B 2
Refused: an option not listed above (including
.B \-\-host
and
.BR \-\-machine ),
a pattern outside
.BR cti\-agent\-* ,
a pattern with other characters, or
.BR \-h .
.TP
.I other
.BR systemctl 's
own exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Every active fleet timer, last and next run:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-timers
.EE
.PP
Including timers that are off, for one lane:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-timers \-\-all 'cti\-agent\-domains*'
.EE
.PP
A refused request, as the orchestrator would see it:
.PP
.EX
$ fleet\-timers \-\-host=user@box
fleet\-timers: '\-\-host=user@box' is not accepted (only \-\-all)
usage: fleet\-timers [\-\-all] [cti\-agent\-PATTERN ...]
$ echo $?
2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
fleet\-kit/fleet/CLAUDE.md; SECURITY.md, "What the orchestrator may run".
